A||||8||mshill||Anyone else use 1200 ceramic based paint?||||5||
Z||000000||mshill||03-15-2000||01:14 PM||||While doing my rebuild I painted my manifolds, y-pipe, and EGR valve base with some 1200 degree ceramic based paint.  When I started it up for the first time and got it warm all of the painted surfaces started smoking like crazy.  I know this stuff requires heat to cure but the smoke seemed excessive.  <P>If anyone else has used this paint, let me know what your experiences have been.||||reg||5
Z||000001||DRH||03-15-2000||03:40 PM||||I've tried different "high heat" paints over the years on different cars. Anything on the exhaust within a couple of feet of the head has always burned off in the first few hours of operation.<P>To be fair... Most say to bake in an <B>oven</B> at 500 or 600 degress for an hour or two for proper curing. I've never had the proper facilities to do that.||||reg||9
Z||000002||mshill||03-15-2000||05:09 PM||||DRH, I hope that is not the case.  But if it is I'm going right to Kragens and start bitching about it.  I see the stuff in just about every autoparts store too.  <P>Good point about the oven curing.  I wonder how many people they sell the stuff to really have the facilities to cure it properly?<P>I should have saved a few bucks an just thrown the dirty ugly stuff back on without painting it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gokart||03-15-2000||07:44 PM||||I used the stuff! Dupli-color, right?! Used it on the heat shields surrounding the pickup coil and other shields around that area. It's been about 6 months and it's held up fine. No smoking, but I could smell it "cooking" for a while!||||reg||14
Z||000004||kong||03-16-2000||01:42 PM||||Curing the paint in an oven does work with the ceramic and powder coat paints. A word of caution, Only use an ELECTRIC oven to do this. While curing, these paints will have flammable chemicals coming off them, so you DON'T want open flames. <BR>  Also you never want to use the stove for food prep after you do this.I have a friend who bought an electric stove for 10 bucks at a garage sale. He put it in his garage and has  done 2 sets of stock exhaust manifolds, and several small parts in powdercoat, and has had very good results. You are only limited to what will fit in the oven.<BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||03-16-2000||02:11 PM||||Well, I ran in my freshly rebuilt engine yesterday after work and the smoking stopped.  They aren't a pretty flat black anymore though.  The color has turned to more of a dark gray.  Later in the evening when everything cooled I rubbed my finger along the manifold and no ash came off so I guess the paint is going to stick, just not as sweet looking as I had hoped.||||reg||1
Z||000006||peterh||03-16-2000||04:05 PM||||I read somewhere else that you can use an old gas bbq grill to heat up your ceramic coatings. Just do not use it to cook any food in after that!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||03-17-2000||08:47 PM||||Paints cannot release all of the thinners during one high temp blast,you have to do it in cycles.If you have a cam to break in also,you'll probably lose the paint during that time,start with a few seconds,or until you can smell it,then let it cool down again.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||03-17-2000||09:11 PM||||I bet that explains why guys like Gokart and I have had such good luck with just painting the shields and brackets.  They don't get all the heat at once and have a better shot a curing.||||reg||1
